The Segment Anything Model (SAM) family provides a powerful foundation for general-purpose image segmentation, yet substantial domain gaps still limit its reliability in medical scenarios. Existing SAM-assisted semi-supervised methods often transfer limited labeled supervision directly to unlabeled targets, which can be difficult when large semantic differences exist between the labeled samples and the targets. We propose RouteSAM, a semantic routing and cross-image propagation framework for semi-supervised 2D medical image segmentation. RouteSAM exploits the latent semantic continuity within abundant unlabeled data to identify intermediate samples that gradually connect a labeled anchor to a queried target, decomposing a difficult direct supervision transfer into a sequence of smoother semantic transitions. Under a fixed annotation budget, representative samples with broad coverage of the training distribution are first selected as labeled anchors. For each target, lesion-aware cross-image matching identifies a suitable anchor and several semantically related unlabeled bridges, forming an ordered Anchor--Bridge--Target route. SAM3 progressively propagates segmentation information along the route to generate cross-image pseudo supervision. To further reduce domain bias and propagation errors, we introduce a multi-stage generalist--specialist collaborative strategy, where a task-specific U-Net audits propagated supervision and guides SAM3 adaptation, followed by re-propagation along the same routes to refine pseudo labels. Semantic routing is used only during training, while final models perform direct single-image inference without reference retrieval or route construction. Experiments on Kvasir-SEG, BUSI, and ISIC2018 demonstrate the effectiveness of RouteSAM under extremely limited annotation budgets, \textbf{[final quantitative results to be inserted]}. 
